\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Definition}
\title{Intrinsic trace on a finite free module}
\date{2026-10-29}

\p{A trace can be defined before choosing a pairing or displaying a
matrix. Let #{k} be a commutative unital ring and #{M} a finite free
#{k}-module. A \newvocab{finite dual frame} is a finite family
#{m_i\in M}, #{\ell_i\in M^\vee} such that
##{x=\sum_i\ell_i(x)m_i\qquad(x\in M).}
A basis and its coefficient functionals supply such a frame; the
counting carrier #{k^V} uses its [delta dual frame](fdip-001F).
For an endomorphism #{T:M\to M}, define its \newvocab{intrinsic trace}
by
##{\trace_M(T)=\sum_i\ell_i(Tm_i).}}

\p{Frame independence, linearity, rectangular cyclicity and conjugacy
are \ref{fdip-00KF}. The [basis realization](fdip-00KG) recovers the
diagonal sum. The finite-free duality convention is
\citet{More on Algebra, Lemma 74.1}{stacks2017stacks},
[Tag 0FNJ](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0FNJ).
The category here is finite free modules over a commutative unital
ring, including the zero module and the zero ring.}
